---
import { alternatives, litepodFacts, reviewed } from "../../data/alternatives.mjs";
import ComparePage from "../../layouts/ComparePage.astro";

const site = Astro.site ?? new URL("https://litepod.sh");
const entries = Object.entries(alternatives);
const schema = [
	{
		"@type": "ItemList",
		name: "Litepod comparisons",
		itemListElement: entries.map(([key, alt], i) => ({
			"@type": "ListItem",
			position: i + 1,
			name: `${alt.name} alternative`,
			url: new URL(`/alternatives/${key}`, site).href,
		})),
	},
];
---

<ComparePage
	title="Self-Hosted Alternatives to Vercel, Coolify and Dokploy | Litepod"
	h1="Self-hosted PaaS alternatives"
	description="Compare Litepod with Vercel, Coolify and Dokploy. Deploy apps and databases on your own VPS with a lightweight, rootless self-hosted PaaS."
	reviewed={reviewed}
	crumbs={[{ name: "Alternatives", path: "/alternatives" }]}
	schema={schema}>
	<p class="text-lp-ink">
		Litepod is a self-hosted PaaS: you install it on a VPS or server you control and deploy applications, Docker
		Compose stacks and databases from one panel. It is an alternative to managed platforms like Vercel, Heroku,
		Render and Railway, and to other self-hosted tools like Coolify, Dokploy and CapRover.
	</p>

	<h2>Compare Litepod</h2>
	<ul>
		{
			entries.map(([key, alt]) => (
				<li>
					<a href={`/alternatives/${key}`}>{alt.h1}</a>: {alt.description}
				</li>
			))
		}
	</ul>

	<h2>What you get with Litepod</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Hosting:</strong> {litepodFacts.hosting}.</li>
		<li><strong>Deploy from:</strong> {litepodFacts.deploy}.</li>
		<li><strong>Databases:</strong> {litepodFacts.databases}.</li>
		<li><strong>Runtime:</strong> {litepodFacts.runtime}.</li>
		<li><strong>SSL:</strong> {litepodFacts.proxy}.</li>
		<li><strong>Footprint:</strong> {litepodFacts.footprint}.</li>
		<li><strong>AI:</strong> {litepodFacts.ai}.</li>
		<li><strong>Managed option:</strong> {litepodFacts.managed}.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Managed PaaS or self-hosted PaaS?</h2>
	<p>
		A managed PaaS runs your code on the vendor's servers and bills for usage. A self-hosted PaaS runs on your own
		VPS: you pay a flat server price, keep your data where you choose, and can run long-lived processes and databases
		next to your apps. The trade-off is that you operate the server, its backups and its monitoring.
	</p>
</ComparePage>
